## Architectural Analysis Guide

### Key Observations for Diagram Generation:

1. **Module Dependencies**: 
   - Analyze import statements across files to understand module coupling
   - Identify which modules depend on others

2. **Class Hierarchies**: 
   - Identify class definitions and their relationships
   - Map out class attributes and methods

3. **Data Flow**: 
   - Trace how data moves through the system
   - Identify data transformations and processing

4. **Entry Points**: 
   - Identify main execution files
   - Map user interaction entry points

5. **Component Relationships**:
   - Show which components interact with each other
   - Identify data passing and method calls

### Suggested Diagram Elements:

#### Components to Include:
- Module relationships
- Data flow paths
- User interaction points

#### Relationships to Show:
- Method calls and data flow
- User interactions and system responses

#### Architectural Layers:
- **Presentation Layer**: User interface and interaction handling
- **Business Logic Layer**: Core application logic and classes
- **Data Layer**: Data models and storage mechanisms
- **Utility Layer**: Helper functions and utilities

## Diagram Generation Instructions

Based on the code analysis above, create a comprehensive {{diagramType}} diagram:

{{#if (eq diagramType "uml")}}
### UML Class Diagram Requirements:
- Show all classes with their attributes and methods
- Display relationships (inheritance, composition, association, aggregation, dependency)
- Include multiplicities where relevant (1, *, 0..1, 1..*, etc.)
- Show visibility modifiers (public +, private -, protected #)
- Group classes into packages if applicable
- Include interfaces and abstract classes with proper notation
- Display class stereotypes where appropriate
- Show method parameters and return types

### Visual Recommendations:
- Use standard UML notation and symbols
- Group related classes into packages/namespaces
- Position base classes above derived classes
- Show directionality with arrows (open for dependency, closed for association)
- Label all relationships clearly
- Use different line styles (solid, dashed) appropriately
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq diagramType "flowchart")}}
### Flowchart Diagram Requirements:
- Show the main execution flow of the system
- Display decision points with diamond shapes
- Include start and end points
- Show all major processes and operations
- Display loops and conditional branches
- Include error handling paths
- Show data inputs and outputs
- Display user interaction points

### Visual Recommendations:
- Use standard flowchart symbols (rectangles for processes, diamonds for decisions)
- Maintain consistent flow direction (typically top-to-bottom or left-to-right)
- Label all decision branches clearly (Yes/No, True/False)
- Group related processes in swim lanes if applicable
- Use different colors for different types of operations
- Keep the flow logical and easy to follow
- Avoid crossing lines where possible
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq diagramType "sequence")}}
### Sequence Diagram Requirements:
- Show main user interaction flows and system responses
- Display all participants/actors at the top
- Show method calls and messages in chronological order
- Include return messages where relevant
- Display activation boxes for active processes
- Show synchronous and asynchronous calls
- Include loops and conditional fragments (alt, opt, loop)
- Display lifelines for all objects
- Show object creation and destruction if applicable

### Visual Recommendations:
- Arrange participants logically (user on left, external systems on right)
- Use solid arrows for synchronous calls, dashed for returns
- Label all messages with method names and parameters
- Include alt/opt/loop frames for conditional logic
- Show timing constraints if relevant
- Keep message flows clear and non-overlapping
- Add notes for complex interactions
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq diagramType "erd")}}
### Entity Relationship Diagram (ERD) Requirements:
- Show all entities (tables/models) in the system
- Display all attributes for each entity
- Mark primary keys (PK) and foreign keys (FK)
- Show relationships between entities (one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many)
- Include cardinality and participation constraints
- Display data types for attributes
- Show unique constraints and indexes
- Include weak entities if applicable

### Visual Recommendations:
- Use standard ERD notation (Chen, Crow's Foot, or UML style)
- Position related entities close to each other
- Clearly mark primary keys (underline or PK label)
- Show foreign key relationships with appropriate lines
- Label relationships with verb phrases
- Use different shapes for entities, attributes, and relationships
- Include a legend for notation used
- Group entities by domain/module if applicable
{{/if}}

